WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.185
Before we start, I want
you to do one short thing.

2
00:00:04.645 --> 00:00:09.405
In the last lesson you picked a format
for every video in your course.

3
00:00:09.825 --> 00:00:14.135
Today you have everything you
need to record the first one.

4
00:00:14.565 --> 00:00:17.875
Open the slides you built in module three.

5
00:00:18.225 --> 00:00:20.335
Grab a blank page,

6
00:00:20.555 --> 00:00:25.065
and write the first five sentences
you will say when you hit record.

7
00:00:25.545 --> 00:00:28.255
Exactly as you will say them.

8
00:00:28.645 --> 00:00:31.580
Pause the video now, you have two minutes.

9
00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:38.665
By the end of this lesson
you can do four things.

10
00:00:39.075 --> 00:00:42.460
Name the six principles of a video lesson.

11
00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:46.830
Spot the open, the body and
the close in any script.

12
00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:50.975
Write a five to ten minute
script for your own lesson.

13
00:00:51.335 --> 00:00:55.920
And check that script against a
ten-point list before you record.

14
00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:58.020
Three parts.

15
00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:01.040
We start with the six principles.

16
00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:02.890
Part one.

17
00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:05.230
Six principles.

18
00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:10.615
For each one I give you a warning
sign, so you can check your own videos.

19
00:01:11.015 --> 00:01:12.850
Here are the six.

20
00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:16.160
One video teaches one idea.

21
00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:20.320
Pictures add to your words,
they do not repeat them.

22
00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:24.510
Cut everything that does
not serve the objective.

23
00:01:24.910 --> 00:01:27.995
Point at exactly what
you are talking about.

24
00:01:28.355 --> 00:01:32.465
Talk to one person, as if they
were sitting next to you.

25
00:01:32.895 --> 00:01:36.605
And make the learner do
something, not just listen.

26
00:01:37.035 --> 00:01:39.395
You just watched two clips.

27
00:01:39.785 --> 00:01:43.120
What does clip A have
that clip B does not?

28
00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:49.360
A wall of text read word for word,
music, fly-ins, and not one question.

29
00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:52.180
Clip B is the opposite.

30
00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:58.095
One idea, a picture that adds to the words,
and a pause where you have to think.

31
00:01:58.585 --> 00:02:00.845
Look at the last column.

32
00:02:01.225 --> 00:02:04.910
Only listening, people
keep about twenty percent.

33
00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:07.930
Listening and watching, fifty.

34
00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:10.865
Doing something with it, ninety.

35
00:02:11.575 --> 00:02:15.210
So what is your video
asking the learner to do?

36
00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:17.575
Part two.

37
00:02:17.865 --> 00:02:20.550
The shape of a single video.

38
00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:25.155
The open has three jobs, and
thirty seconds to do them.

39
00:02:25.515 --> 00:02:30.950
A hook, a mistake everyone makes, so
the learner wants to keep watching.

40
00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:35.150
A promise, what they can do
by the end of the video.

41
00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.625
And the route, the two
or three stops ahead.

42
00:02:39.015 --> 00:02:42.075
No long hello. No life story.

43
00:02:42.845 --> 00:02:45.405
Here is how the time splits.

44
00:02:45.765 --> 00:02:48.625
The open takes about ten percent.

45
00:02:48.985 --> 00:02:54.995
The body takes three quarters, two to four
ideas, each with an example and a pause.

46
00:02:55.515 --> 00:03:01.300
The close takes the last fifteen percent,
three takeaways and one thing to do next.

47
00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:03.285
Part three.

48
00:03:03.635 --> 00:03:06.245
Six steps to a finished script.

49
00:03:06.665 --> 00:03:08.300
Six steps.

50
00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:11.720
Fix the objective in one sentence.

51
00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.390
Cut the lesson into eight
to fifteen segments.

52
00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:18.805
Write what you will say, in spoken words.

53
00:03:19.155 --> 00:03:21.940
Pair each segment with one slide.

54
00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.055
Add at least two pauses where
the learner does something.

55
00:03:26.465 --> 00:03:29.175
Then read it out loud and time it.

56
00:03:29.575 --> 00:03:31.035
Quick check.

57
00:03:31.355 --> 00:03:34.840
Your script runs twelve
minutes when you read it aloud.

58
00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:36.975
What do you do?

59
00:03:38.045 --> 00:03:41.430
Pause the video and pick
one, thirty seconds.

60
00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:49.255
The answer is B.

61
00:03:49.625 --> 00:03:52.985
Over ten minutes, cut or split the video.

62
00:03:53.415 --> 00:03:56.875
Talking faster only loses the learner.

63
00:03:57.295 --> 00:03:59.905
Three keywords for this lesson.

64
00:04:00.285 --> 00:04:05.820
Six principles: one idea per video,
and pictures that add to your words.

65
00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:09.762
Open, body, close: a hook
in thirty seconds, an

66
00:04:09.802 --> 00:04:13.050
example in the body, an
action at the close.

67
00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:19.175
Read it aloud: a script is done only when
you have read it out loud and checked it.

68
00:04:19.865 --> 00:04:24.475
Remember those three and you can
write your first script tonight.

69
00:04:24.895 --> 00:04:26.655
Your next step.

70
00:04:27.005 --> 00:04:31.940
Open the script template and write the
script for one lesson of your course.

71
00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:36.770
Read it aloud, time it, then check
it against the ten-point list.

72
00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.385
You have seven days.

73
00:04:39.765 --> 00:04:44.200
In the next lesson we record
and edit exactly this script.
